<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The macOS half of the host-process deployment. genproj emits no deploy/, so
  this file is authored here (phase-1 plan, section 2.2).

  What is supervised is the LAUNCHER, not the agent (hard constraint #14): the
  fetch has to happen before the agent starts, and the launcher ends in `exec`,
  so from launchd's point of view launcher and payload are one process.

  Install (per user, no sudo):

      # 1. Cold start: the launcher is a release asset, not a file in a checkout.
      dir="$HOME/.local/share/a2a-goose"; mkdir -p "$dir"
      curl -fsSL https://github.com/nickbrett1/a2a-goose/releases/latest/download/fetch-launch.sh \
        -o "$dir/fetch-launch.sh"
      chmod +x "$dir/fetch-launch.sh"

      # 2. The agent, supervised; it starts and supervises goose itself.
      mkdir -p ~/Library/LaunchAgents ~/Library/Logs/a2a-goose
      cp deploy/launchd/com.nick.a2a-goose.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
      launchctl bootstrap gui/"$(id -u)" ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.nick.a2a-goose.plist
      launchctl print gui/"$(id -u)"/com.nick.a2a-goose | head

  Stop it:

      launchctl bootout gui/"$(id -u)"/com.nick.a2a-goose

  Joining the roost fleet (mission control). A host appears in the fleet only
  by dialling the hub, which needs a `hub:` block in its config.yaml and
  A2A_GOOSE_HUB_TOKEN in ENV_FILE. Both files are hand-authored here, so deploy/
  never regenerates them; ensure-hub.sh only ADDS what is missing and is
  safe to re-run, as the user that owns goose:

      /path/to/a2a-goose/deploy/ensure-hub.sh

  launchd runs one program and cannot chain, and this unit deliberately has no
  shell wrapper, so the wiring is a deploy step rather than something this file
  can do at load. Run it once at install; re-run it after any host rebuild.

  Adding the block is only half of it: the running BINARY must also have hub
  code, and an old one does not. mac-studio proved the trap - this job ran
  a2a-goose 0.1.41 (no hub code at all) since 2026-09-17, registered with
  LiteLLM on every start, and never dialled roost. The restart is what fixes
  that, because the launcher fetches the newest release on every start:

      kill -TERM <payload pid>     # graceful; KeepAlive relaunches -> fetch-launch -> current release
      launchctl kickstart gui/"$(id -u)"/com.nick.a2a-goose   # only when the job is already down

  Do NOT use `launchctl kickstart -k`: `-k` is a SIGKILL and orphans the goose
  child (RUNBOOK.md). Editing the config or env is not a restart and pulls
  nothing - the job has to come back up for the fetch to run.

  There is no ordering dependency on the network. launchd has no
  network-online equivalent, and it does not need one: the launcher fails open
  (hard constraint #13), so a boot with no network installs what is already
  there and starts it. The fetch that matters happens on the next restart.

  There is no shell wrapper and no environment file here on purpose. Everything
  the payload needs - the bearer token, LITELLM_BASE_URL, the bind address -
  comes from ENV_FILE ($HOME/.config/a2a-goose/env, mode 0600), which the
  launcher sources on its way to the exec. Secrets stay on the host; the release
  payload never carries them.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.nick.a2a-goose</string>

  <!-- The launcher, at the path the cold start above installed it. It is a
       release asset, not a file in a checkout: every start it replaces itself
       with whatever the newest release advertises, so the path is fixed and the
       code behind it is not. Nothing here may point back at a repository -
       that is what left this host running a stale launcher (S11). -->
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/Users/nick/.local/share/a2a-goose/fetch-launch.sh</string>
  </array>

  <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
  <string>/Users/nick/.local/share/a2a-goose</string>

  <!-- goose has to be resolvable from this PATH: the agent's first act is to
       verify it (src/goose.rs), and with goose.acp.serve: own the agent then
       spawns it - the child inherits this environment, so this PATH is the one
       the ACP server is started from. Both install locations are listed because
       the goose installer and Homebrew disagree about where goose goes. -->
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>PATH</key>
    <string>/Users/nick/.local/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
  </dict>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- Restart on any exit, which is also what makes a crash-restart re-run the
       fetch: the payload is `exec`ed, so its exit is this process's exit.

       The agent starts and stops its own `goose serve` underneath this, which is
       the other half of the boot story: launchd supervises the agent, the agent
       supervises goose. launchd's default (AbandonProcessGroup unset) is to kill
       the job's remaining processes when the job exits, which is the backstop
       for an agent that was SIGKILLed and never got to stop its child. -->
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- launchd's own floor between restarts. It is the backstop for a payload
       that dies instantly on load, which is the failure S12 exists to catch
       before a host ever sees a release. -->
  <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
  <integer>30</integer>

  <key>ProcessType</key>
  <string>Background</string>

  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/Users/nick/Library/Logs/a2a-goose/launcher.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/Users/nick/Library/Logs/a2a-goose/launcher.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
